# TCP vs UDP: Key Differences and When to Use Each

## Core Differences

| Aspect | TCP | UDP |
|--------|-----|-----|
| **Connection** | Connection-oriented (3-way handshake) | Connectionless |
| **Reliability** | Guaranteed delivery, ordering, and integrity | Best-effort; no guarantees |
| **Header size** | 20–60 bytes | 8 bytes |
| **Speed** | Slower (overhead from ACKs, retransmits) | Faster (minimal overhead) |
| **Flow/Congestion control** | Yes (windowing, slow start, etc.) | No |
| **Ordering** | Packets reassembled in order | No ordering guarantee |
| **Error checking** | Checksum + retransmission | Checksum only (no retransmit) |
| **Broadcast/Multicast** | Not supported | Supported |
| **Data stream** | Byte stream | Datagrams (message boundaries preserved) |

## How They Work

**TCP** establishes a connection via a three-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK), numbers every segment, acknowledges receipt, retransmits lost data, and tears down gracefully. This makes it behave like a reliable pipe.

**UDP** just fires datagrams at an address. If one is lost or arrives out of order, the application must handle it (or ignore it).

